  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 429 AM MST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nIR imagery early this morning shows an approaching trough closing in \non Wyoming, with a cloud shield now extending from southern Nevada \nthrough Idaho. This will bring a welcome return of moisture to the \narea, starting first across western Wyoming. Rain and mountain snow \nwill move in late this morning, spreading northeast through the day. \nThis will mostly be limited to areas along and west of the \nContinental Divide through sunset. Locations east of the Divide will \nsee one more warm day, with temperatures peaking 10 to near 20 \ndegrees above normal. But for most of these areas, this will also \ncome with a wind tax. The entire wind corridor, from Sweetwater \nCounty through southern Johnson County, can expect southwesterly \ngusts of 30 to 45 mph this afternoon. This will come ahead of a cold \nfront that will push into western Wyoming around sunset. This will \nlower snow levels across the west down to valley bottoms tonight. \nMost of the snow will remain in the mountains, however, particularly \nacross the Tetons, southwestern Yellowstone National Park, and the \nwestern slopes of the Wind River Range. Accumulations here are now \nin the 5 to 10 inch range above 9,000 feet. Winter Weather \nAdvisories are now in effect for portions of these areas, most \nnotably covering Teton and Togwotee passes which could see 3 to 6 \ninches. Snowfall rates will be heaviest ahead of the front this \nafternoon as upper level lift is maximized, as well as behind the \nfront tonight as upslope flow increases. With this afternoon's \nprecipitation falling as rain across the western valleys, \naccumulating snow will be limited, with only 1 to 2 inches expected \nby Wednesday morning.\n\nMeanwhile, wind will ramp up further late this evening along and \neast of the Divide as a surface low develops across northern Wyoming \nahead of the advancing cold front. This will lead to gusts of 55 to \n65 mph across the more susceptible areas of the Wind Corridor, \nthough gusts of 35 to 45 will be more widespread elsewhere. High \nWind Warnings are now in effect for the strongest wind gusts, most \nnotably in the Casper area.\n\nPrecipitation chances increase east of the Divide tonight, and with \nthe cold front some locations may even find a light dusting of snow \nWednesday morning. Overall, moisture across the basins is expected \nto be low, generally under a tenth of an inch. The Bighorns will \nfare better, with accumulating snowfall expected tonight through \nWednesday morning. Forecast snow totals across the higher elevations \nhere have been increasing as of late (4 to 8 inches). This bears \nwatching for a possible additional Winter Weather Advisory; \nconfidence on totals was not high enough to issue one this morning. \n\nOne other area to watch will be the I-80 Corridor in Sweetwater \nCounty after sunset tonight. There will be enough instability for \nshowers and even a few thunderstorms this afternoon and evening, \nwith most of the associated precipitation falling as rain. However, \ntemperatures will drop with the front, which could lead to some snow \nsquall activity late this evening and early Wednesday morning. \nModels have been picking up on the potential for light snow up \nto an inch with this activity. This could lead to some travel \nimpacts tonight along I-80, especially when combined with the \ngusty winds. \n\nPrecipitation will come to an end everywhere by late Wednesday \nmorning. A cooler airmass will keep temperatures much more \nseasonable with highs behind the system in the 30s and 40s. The next \nsystem will follow quickly, with most models bringing it into \nwestern Wyoming Thursday morning and spreading precipitation chances \nacross remaining areas. Forecast precipitation totals have not \nchanged much, though perhaps trended slightly down as of late, with \nmost valleys and basins forecast to see less than a tenth of an \ninch. Regardless, cooler and more unsettled conditions are expected \nto continue through this weekend.",
